[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m First, let's determine the time it takes for each gear to complete one full rotation.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m For the first gear, which turns \(33\frac{1}{3}\) times in a minute:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m \[ 33\frac{1}{3} = \frac{100}{3} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m So, the first gear completes \(\frac{100}{3}\) rotations per minute. Therefore, the time for one full rotation is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m \[ \text{Time for one rotation} = \frac{3}{100} \text{ minutes} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m For the second gear, which turns 45 times in a minute:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m \[ \text{Time for one rotation} = \frac{1}{45} \text{ minutes} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m Next, we need to find the LCM of \(\frac{3}{100}\) and \(\frac{1}{45}\). To do this, we first find the LCM of the numerators (3 and 1) and the GCD (Greatest Common Divisor) of the denominators (100 and 45).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m The LCM of 3 and 1 is 3.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m The GCD of 100 and 45 is 5.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m Thus, the LCM of \(\frac{3}{100}\) and \(\frac{1}{45}\) is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m \[ \text{LCM} = \frac{\text{LCM of numerators}}{\text{GCD of denominators}} = \frac{3}{5} \text{ minutes} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m Now, we convert this time into seconds:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m \[ \frac{3}{5} \text{ minutes} = \frac{3}{5} \times 60 \text{ seconds} = 36 \text{ seconds} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 
