[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 7. \( 17^2 = 289 \)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 8. \( 19^2 = 361 \)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 9. \( 23^2 = 529 \)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 10. \( 29^2 = 841 \)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 11. \( 31^2 = 961 \)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m Next, we need to check if there are any prime numbers \( p \) such that \( p^2 \geq 1000 \). The next prime number after 31 is 37, and \( 37^2 = 1369 \), which is greater than 1000. Therefore, we do not need to consider any prime numbers greater than 31.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m Thus, the natural numbers less than 1000 that have exactly three distinct positive integer divisors are the squares of the prime numbers listed above. Counting these, we have 11 such numbers.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 
