[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m The key insight is that the sum of the squares of the distances from $P$ to the vertices of one pyramid is equal to the sum of the squares of the distances from $P$ to the vertices of the other pyramid. This is due to the symmetry of the octahedron.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m Let's denote the unknown distance as $x$. We have the distances 3, 7, 8, 9, and 11, and we need to find $x$. We can pair the distances such that the sum of the squares of the distances from $P$ to the vertices of one pyramid equals the sum of the squares of the distances from $P$ to the vertices of the other pyramid.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 
