[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m [response] A regular octahedron can be thought of as two pyramids with a square base joined at the base. The vertices of the octahedron can be divided into two groups of four, each group being equidistant from the center of the octahedron. If we consider the point $P$ and its distances to the vertices, the sum of the squares of the distances from $P$ to the vertices of the octahedron is constant. This is because the vertices of the octahedron are symmetrically placed around the center.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m Let the distance from $P$ to the sixth vertex be $x$. The sum of the squares of the distances from $P$ to the vertices of the octahedron is constant. Therefore, we have:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m \[3^2 + 7^2 + 8^2 + 9^2 + 11^2 + x^2 = \text{constant}\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m The constant can be calculated by considering the symmetry of the octahedron. Since the sum of the squares of the distances from $P$ to the vertices is the same for any point $P$, we can use the known distances to find $x$:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m \[9 + 49 + 64 + 81 + 121 + x^2 = 2 \times (3^2 + 7^2 + 8^2 + 9^2 + 11^2 - x^2)\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m \[324 + x^2 = 2 \times (324 - x^2)\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m \[324 + x^2 = 648 - 2x^2\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m \[3x^2 = 324\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m \[x^2 = 108\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m \[x = \sqrt{108} = 6\sqrt{3}\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m Therefore, the distance from $P$ to the sixth vertex is \boxed{6\sqrt{3}}.
