[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m The first gear thus turns $\frac{100}{3}$ times per minute, and the second gear turns $45$ times per minute. To find the time when both marks will again point due north, we need to find the Least Common Multiple (LCM) of the periods of each rotation.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m First, convert these rotations per minute into periods (time per rotation):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m - First gear: $1 \div \frac{100}{3} = \frac{3}{100}$ minutes per rotation.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m - Second gear: $1 \div 45 = \frac{1}{45}$ minutes per rotation.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m To find the LCM of $\frac{3}{100}$ and $\frac{1}{45}$, we actually find the LCM of the numerators and GCD (effectively, aligning denominators):
