[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m [response] To solve this problem, we need to understand the geometric interpretation of the complex numbers involved. The distance from the starting point to the last house on both days is given as $\sqrt{2012}$. On Saturday, Eli starts at $z$ and ends at $z^{2013}$, while on Sunday, he starts at $1$ and ends at $z^{2012}$. The distance formula in the complex plane is the modulus of the difference between the two points.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m For Saturday, the distance is $|z^{2013} - z| = |z||z^{2012} - 1| = \sqrt{2012}$. For Sunday, the distance is $|z^{2012} - 1| = \sqrt{2012}$. This implies $|z| = 1$, meaning $z$ lies on the unit circle. Therefore, $z = e^{i\theta}$ for some angle $\theta$.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1536333)[0m Since $|z^{2012} - 1| = \sqrt{2012}$, we can infer that $z^{2012}$ is a point on the unit circle that is $\sqrt{2012}$ away from $1$. This means $z^{2012}$ is at an angle where the real part is $-2011/2012$ (since the distance formula in the complex plane for a unit circle point $(\cos \phi, \sin \phi)$ from $(1,0)$ is $\sqrt{2 - 2\cos \phi}$, and solving $2 - 2\cos \phi = 2012/2012$ gives $\cos \phi = -2011/2012$).
